{"task_id": "Mbpp/57", "solution": "import functools\ndef find_Max_Num(listOfIntegers: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the largest number that can be formed by concatenating a given list of digits.\n    Converts digits to strings, sorts them using a custom comparison, and joins the result.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not listOfIntegers:\n        return 0\n\n    string_digits = [str(num) for num in listOfIntegers]\n\n    # Custom comparison logic: s1 comes before s2 if s1s2 forms a larger number than s2s1.\n    # This ensures a descending sort based on the effective value for concatenation.\n    def compare_digits(s1, s2):\n        if s1 + s2 > s2 + s1:\n            return -1  # s1 is effectively 'larger', so it should come first.\n        elif s1 + s2 < s2 + s1:\n            return 1   # s1 is effectively 'smaller', so it should come after s2.\n        else:\n            return 0\n\n    sorted_digits = sorted(string_digits, key=functools.cmp_to_key(compare_digits))\n\n    # Join the sorted digits to form the final largest number string.\n    # int() handles leading zeros correctly, e.g., \"00\" becomes 0.\n    result_str = \"\".join(sorted_digits)\n    return int(result_str)"}
