{"task_id": "Mbpp/247", "solution": "def lps(inputString: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the length of the longest palindromic subsequence in the given string.\n    This implementation uses dynamic programming to find the maximum length.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(inputString)\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # dp[i][j] stores the length of the longest palindromic subsequence of inputString[i...j]\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n\n    # All single characters are palindromic subsequences of length 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i][i] = 1\n\n    # Fill the DP table for lengths from 2 to n\n    for length in range(2, n + 1):\n        for i in range(n - length + 1):\n            j = i + length - 1\n            if inputString[i] == inputString[j]:\n                # If characters match, add 2 plus the LPS of the substring between them.\n                # If length is 2 (e.g., \"aa\"), the inner subsequence is empty, so add 0.\n                dp[i][j] = 2 + (dp[i+1][j-1] if length > 2 else 0)\n            else:\n                # If characters don't match, take the maximum of excluding inputString[i] or inputString[j].\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i+1][j], dp[i][j-1])\n\n    return dp[0][n-1]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/247", "solution": "def lps(input_string: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the length of the longest palindromic subsequence using dynamic programming.\n    It fills a 2D DP table to store LPS lengths of substrings.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(input_string)\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # dp[i][j] will store the length of LPS of substring input_string[i...j]\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n\n    # All single characters are palindromes of length 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i][i] = 1\n\n    # Fill the table for substrings of length 2 to n\n    for length in range(2, n + 1):\n        for i in range(n - length + 1):\n            j = i + length - 1  # End index of the current substring\n\n            if input_string[i] == input_string[j]:\n                if length == 2:\n                    dp[i][j] = 2\n                else:\n                    dp[i][j] = 2 + dp[i + 1][j - 1]\n            else:\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i + 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])\n\n    return dp[0][n - 1]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/244", "solution": "import math\ndef next_Perfect_Square(inputNumber: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the smallest perfect square strictly greater than the given integer.\n    For negative inputs, it returns 0 as the next perfect square.\n    \"\"\"\n    if inputNumber < 0:\n        return 0\n    else:\n        # Calculate the integer square root (floor) of the inputNumber.\n        # Adding 1 to this result yields the integer root for the next perfect square.\n        next_root = math.isqrt(inputNumber) + 1\n        return next_root * next_root"}
