{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "def max_Product(numbers: list[int]) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds a pair with the highest product from a given array of integers.\n    It sorts the array and compares the product of the two largest numbers\n    with the product of the two smallest numbers to find the maximum.\n    \"\"\"\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    # Calculate product of the two largest numbers\n    product_of_largest = sorted_numbers[-1] * sorted_numbers[-2]\n    pair_of_largest = (sorted_numbers[-1], sorted_numbers[-2])\n\n    # Calculate product of the two smallest numbers (which could be two negative numbers\n    # yielding a large positive product)\n    product_of_smallest = sorted_numbers[0] * sorted_numbers[1]\n    pair_of_smallest = (sorted_numbers[0], sorted_numbers[1])\n\n    if product_of_largest >= product_of_smallest:\n        return pair_of_largest\n    else:\n        return pair_of_smallest"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/418", "solution": "def Find_Max(input_list: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Finds the sub-list with the maximum length from a given list of lists.\n    It iterates through the input to identify and return the longest nested list.\"\"\"\n    max_length_sublist = []\n    max_length = 0\n\n    for sub_list in input_list:\n        current_length = len(sub_list)\n        if current_length > max_length:\n            max_length = current_length\n            max_length_sublist = sub_list\n\n    return max_length_sublist"}
