{"task_id": "Mbpp/589", "solution": "import math\ndef perfect_squares(start: int, end: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"Finds all perfect squares within a given inclusive range.\n    Optimizes by calculating square root boundaries and iterating through integer roots.\"\"\"\n\n    # Adjust start to 0 if negative, as perfect squares are non-negative.\n    adjusted_start = max(0, start)\n\n    perfect_squares_list = []\n\n    # Calculate the starting integer root whose square is >= adjusted_start.\n    # math.ceil and math.sqrt return floats, cast to int for range.\n    start_root = math.ceil(math.sqrt(adjusted_start))\n\n    # Calculate the ending integer root whose square is <= end.\n    # If end is negative, sqrt will raise an error or return NaN.\n    # Trusting inputs means end >= 0 for squares to exist.\n    end_root = math.floor(math.sqrt(end))\n\n    # Iterate from start_root to end_root (inclusive) and collect squares.\n    for k in range(int(start_root), int(end_root) + 1):\n        square = k * k\n        perfect_squares_list.append(square)\n\n    return perfect_squares_list"}
